{
  "reviewer": "robustness_auditor",
  "paper_id": "bicycle-infrastructure-paper",
  "run_status": "ok",
  "summary": "The paper is a measurement and pipeline paper with no regression analysis, no preregistration and no formal robustness section. Its robustness-relevant material is the 34-article pilot validation (three relevant articles), the three-run LLM consistency check, descriptive subgroup readings (season, county, ceremony versus no ceremony, time trend in listings) and the keyword classification in Appendix A. I found twelve material problems. The agreement headline is reported without its n=3 caveat and rests on post hoc relabelling and a validation set used for prompt revision. Stability, model and setting sensitivity, and full-run accuracy are untested. Several descriptive subgroup and trend readings are stated more strongly than the displayed evidence supports. The appendix classification rule contradicts its own example. The structured table CSVs were avoided in favour of page text and page images, in line with the parser-quality guidance.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "ROB-001",
      "category": "robustness_overstatement",
      "finding_summary": "The abstract and conclusion say LLM–human relevance agreement is 'the same rate' as and 'matched' human–human agreement, and that the LLM gives the same date for 'all three' articles, without the n=3 'indicative only' caveat stated in Section 4.",
      "issue_type": "manuscript_issue",
      "severity": "medium",
      "confidence": "high",
      "location": {
        "page": 1,
        "page_label": "1",
        "section": "Abstract",
        "text_quote": "on the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders,",
        "precision": "exact"
      },
      "claim_text": "On the pilot, the LLM agrees with a human coder on the relevance of 97 percent of the articles, the same rate as between two human coders, and gives the same opening date for all three relevant articles.",
      "assessment": "partially",
      "cannot_verify_reason": null,
      "evidence_summary": "Tables 3 and 4 have identical cells (3/0/1/30), so raw agreement is 33 of 34 in both. Only 3 pilot-coded articles are relevant and 30 of 34 are agreed negatives. Section 4 says the sample 'is too small to test whether the two agreement rates differ. The results are thus indicative only.' Section 8 repeats that a test is not possible. The abstract, introduction and conclusion carry no such qualifier and say 'matched'. Date agreement (3 of 3 for LLM versus 2 of 3 for the second coder) is a one-article difference. Agreement on positive-class decisions and on date precision is not reported separately, although in Table 4 the LLM flags 4 relevant articles against the pilot coder's 3.",
